Haz360 AI Governance & Budget
1. Budgeting Strategy
•	Recommended Approach: Use Cursor Pro ($20/month flat fee).
o	Why: This eliminates the stress of "token counting." You get a predictable cost, and you are free to experiment without monitoring your usage every hour.
•	API Approach (Optional): If you use the Anthropic API (pay-per-token), log into your Anthropic Console Billing and set a "Usage Limit" (e.g., set to $25.00). The API will stop working once you hit that, preventing surprise bills.
2. Iteration Discipline (The "Architect's Rule")
To avoid wasting tokens and AI "compute," follow the "No-Retry Rule":
1.	Before Prompting: Ensure the context is clear (e.g., "Review the schema.prisma file before generating the form").
2.	The 3-Attempt Cap: If a feature isn't working after 3 attempts, stop. The AI is stuck in a bad context loop.
o	Solution: Create a new chat session in Cursor, copy in the schema.prisma file, and explain the problem from scratch. This is cheaper and faster than fighting with a "broken" chat.
3. Scope Governance
•	Do not ask the AI to "Refactor" or "Optimize" code unless you have a specific performance issue.
•	Focus on Function: If the code works for the Demo, move on. You are building an MVP, not a production-grade enterprise system yet.
4. Operational Checkpoint
•	Before moving to a new phase, verify the current phase is stable. Do not let the AI stack unverified logic on top of itself.


The Chain: Each phase feeds the next. You cannot have a dashboard (Phase 4) without data (Phase 1).
The Guardrails: These prompts instruct the AI to build components that interact with the data, which is exactly how a scalable system is designed.